  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "claim": "The source diff adds an administrator operation for removing disabled projects, a persistent removal marker, current-project filtering, and a console action. The CLI version and health commands, HTTP health and metrics handlers, protocol error handlers and dependency files have no source changes.",
      "status": "observed",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "706fecbc285f0e3e415233aaef2b8b7ac50c4b7b",
        "method": "Exact Git diff against c779976b4b4e95cda9d10a7a19649de6c6ec1bfd and inspection of the changed handlers, store and embedded console."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "claim": "A disposable database produced by the prior binary at schema 26 upgraded to schema 27. After project removal and gateway restart the removed slug stayed reserved, its recorded usage retained its project and key identity, and the surviving project could still use the shared provider connection.",
      "status": "observed",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "go/internal/iam/schema.go, go/internal/iam/project_removal_test.go, go/internal/iam/migration_test.go",
        "method": "Old and candidate binaries exercised over HTTP with a disposable database and local provider fixture, followed by restart and checks of management lists, historical attribution and the surviving project."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "claim": "The administrator endpoint rejects an active project, an unknown ID, a project credential, and non-admin or cross-origin SSO mutations. An authorized same-origin deletion succeeds. A failed audit insert returns a server error and preserves the disabled project. Retrying after recovery records its request ID. Six concurrent HTTP deletions produced one durable audit event.",
      "status": "observed",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "go/internal/api/project_delete_test.go, go/internal/api/audit.go, go/internal/iam/repository.go",
        "method": "Focused HTTP tests with a disposable store, an audit-insert failure fixture and the candidate binary concurrency and restart smoke check."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "claim": "All 285 console tests passed. Consecutive builds produced identical assets, and the candidate binary served the final script byte-for-byte. The console confirmation identifies the project, discloses slug reservation and explains the effect on historical usage and provider connections.",
      "status": "observed",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "go/internal/web/console/src/pages/Access.tsx, go/internal/web/console/tests/project-page.test.mjs, go/internal/web/console/dist/index.html",
        "method": "Console tests, repeated builds, comparison of generated assets and HTTP comparison against the script referenced by the candidate console."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "claim": "The final source passed the complete Go test suite, build, vet and documentation checks. Active projects retained key-scoped model discovery in gateway HTTP fixtures. Authentication, embedded console serving and default-off metrics tests passed in the complete suite.",
      "status": "observed",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "go/internal/api/models_test.go, go/internal/api/sso_test.go, go/internal/api/server_test.go, go/internal/api/observe_test.go, scripts/check-docs.mjs",
        "method": "go test -count=1 ./..., go build ./... and go vet ./... from go/, followed by node scripts/check-docs.mjs from the repository root. The local binary smoke also exercised the surviving project."
      }
    }
  ],
